  <h2>Therapy when you are the one who usually holds it together</h2>
  <p>There is a particular kind of tiredness that belongs to people who look after others for a living. It is not fixed by a weekend. You can sleep for ten hours and still wake up with the sense that something is running in the background, using everything up. Nurses know this one. So do doctors, techs, aides, chaplains, and the administrators who carry the parts nobody sees.</p>
  <p>Natasha worked in that world before she worked in this one, as a registered nurse and later as a healthcare director. She is not guessing at what a shift does to a person, or how strange it is to be endlessly competent at work and quietly falling apart at home. That gap between the composed professional and the person driving home afterwards is often where the work starts.</p>
  <p>And if you are on the other side of it, living with an illness rather than treating one, the same experience applies in reverse. She knows how the system speaks, which means she can help you work out what you were actually told, and what you are allowed to feel about it.</p>
{% endcall %}
